'\" t
.TH V4DUMP 5 "October 28, 2026" "unix4-toolchain" "File Formats Manual"
.ad l
.nh
.SH NAME
v4dump \- v4linux crash dump container format
.SH DESCRIPTION
A
.B v4dump
file is a crash dump container generated by the
.B v4linux
emulator upon receiving an unhandled fatal signal or CPU fault. The container
records complete CPU state, register files, virtual memory segments, process
metadata, execution arguments, signal handlers, and symbol definitions.
.PP
The file version, signal, and other header information can be previewed with the 
\fBfile\fR(1) command, and parsed with \fBv4-dumpinfo\fR(1).
.PP
All multibyte integer fields in the container are stored in little-endian byte
order.
.SH CONTAINER
A
.B v4dump
file consists of:
.IP 1. 3
A fixed 256-byte primary header located at offset 0.
.IP 2. 3
A section header table describing contained data payloads.
.IP 3. 3
Variable-length payload sections (memory pages, tables, and strings), aligned
to 16-byte boundaries.
.SH HEADER
The header occupies the first 256 bytes of the dump:
.PP
.TS
tab(@);
r r l l.
Offset@Size@Type@Field
0@8@char[8]@Magic identifier ("\ex89V4DUMP\en")
8@2@uint16_t@Major format version (1)
10@2@uint16_t@Minor format version (0)
12@4@uint32_t@Header size in bytes (256)
16@4@uint32_t@Total file size in bytes
20@4@uint32_t@CRC32 checksum of file (with this field set to 0)
24@4@uint32_t@Container flags (0x0001: process was forked)
28@4@uint32_t@Number of section headers
32@4@uint32_t@Byte offset to section table
36@4@uint32_t@Section table entry size (16 bytes)
40@8@uint64_t@Crash timestamp (seconds since UNIX epoch)
48@4@uint32_t@Host operating system process ID
52@2@uint16_t@Guest process ID (PID)
54@2@uint16_t@Guest parent process ID (PPID)
56@2@uint16_t@Terminating signal number
58@2@uint16_t@Fault flags (0x0001: fault address is valid)
60@2@uint16_t@Faulting memory address
62@2@uint16_t@Program counter of executing instruction
64@16@uint16_t[8]@PDP-11 registers r0 through r7 (r6=sp, r7=pc)
80@2@uint16_t@Processor status word (ps)
82@2@uint16_t@Executable a.out magic (0407 or 0410)
84@4@uint32_t@Text segment start address
88@4@uint32_t@Text segment length in bytes
92@4@uint32_t@Data segment start address
96@4@uint32_t@Data segment length in bytes
100@4@uint32_t@BSS segment start address
104@4@uint32_t@BSS segment length in bytes
108@4@uint32_t@End address of initial binary image
112@4@uint32_t@Current process break address (brk)
116@4@uint32_t@Lowest backed stack address
120@4@uint32_t@Initial stack top address
124@4@uint32_t@Initial stack pointer (sp) value
128@4@uint32_t@Executable entry address
132@4@uint32_t@Guest address space size (typically 65536)
136@4@uint32_t@Symbol count in symbol table
140@2@uint16_t@Process argument count (argc)
142@114@uint8_t[114]@Reserved; padded with zeroes
.TE
.SH SECTION HEADER
The section header table begins at the offset given by
.I sect_off
in the header. Each entry is 16 bytes long:
.PP
.TS
tab(@);
r r l l.
Offset@Size@Type@Field
0@4@uint32_t@Section four-character identifier tag
4@4@uint32_t@Reserved / alignment padding
8@4@uint32_t@Byte offset from start of file to section data
12@4@uint32_t@Length of section data in bytes
.TE
.SH SECTIONS
The following sections are defined:
.TP
.B MMAP
Virtual memory segment mapping. An array of 16-byte descriptors:
.RS
.IP \(bu 2
.BR "uint32_t start" :
Guest starting address.
.IP \(bu 2
.BR "uint32_t size" :
Region length in bytes.
.IP \(bu 2
.BR "uint32_t file_off" :
Byte offset into the file (or
.B MDAT
section) where region contents are stored.
.IP \(bu 2
.BR "uint16_t kind" :
Segment type: 1 (text), 2 (padding), 3 (data), 4 (bss), 5 (heap), 6
(unmapped), 7 (stack), 8 (arguments).
.IP \(bu 2
.BR "uint16_t flags" :
Flags. If
.B 0x0001
.RB ( V4D_SEG_STORED )
is set, data is present in the
.B MDAT
section.
.RE
.TP
.B MDAT
Memory data payload. Contains the contiguous binary images of memory segments
flagged with
.B V4D_SEG_STORED
in the
.B MMAP
table.
.TP
.B FPU
Floating-point unit state (8-byte header followed by accumulator values):
.RS
.IP \(bu 2
.BR "uint16_t fps" :
Floating-point status register.
.IP \(bu 2
.BR "uint16_t cc" :
FPU condition codes.
.IP \(bu 2
.BR "uint16_t nac" :
Number of recorded 64-bit accumulators (up to
.BR V4D_NAC ,
which is 8 for emulator performance purposes).
.IP \(bu 2
.BR "uint16_t reserved" :
2 bytes of padding for 64-bit alignment.
.IP \(bu 2
.BR "uint64_t ac[nac]" :
Array of
.I nac
floating-point accumulators (
.B ac0
through
.BR ac7 ),
stored as 64-bit IEEE double-precision values.
.RE
.TP
.B SIGS
Signal state (8-byte header followed by handler table):
.RS
.IP \(bu 2
.BR "uint32_t pending" :
Bitmask of pending signals (bits 1 through 31).
.IP \(bu 2
.BR "uint16_t nsigs" :
Number of signal handlers recorded.
.IP \(bu 2
.BR "uint16_t reserved" :
Padding.
.IP \(bu 2
.BR "uint16_t handler[nsigs]" :
Array of handler addresses (0 = default, 1 = ignored, other = guest code
address).
.RE
.TP
.B FDS
Open file descriptor map:
.RS
.IP \(bu 2
.BR "uint32_t nfds" :
Number of file descriptors recorded.
.IP \(bu 2
.BR "int32_t host_fd[nfds]" :
Array of host file descriptors corresponding to guest descriptors 0 through
.IR nfds - 1
(a value of -1 indicates a closed descriptor).
.RE
.TP
.B SYMS
Symbol table. An array of 12-byte entries matching the PDP-11
.B a.out
symbol format:
.RS
.IP \(bu 2
.BR "char name[8]" :
NUL-terminated or NUL-padded symbol name.
.IP \(bu 2
.BR "uint16_t type" :
Symbol type bits (0: undefined, 1: absolute, 2: text, 3: data, 4: bss; bit
040 marks external or global).
.IP \(bu 2
.BR "uint16_t value" :
Symbol address or offset.
.RE
.TP
.B ARGV
Sequence of
.I argc
NUL-terminated strings containing process invocation arguments
(
.B argv[0]
through
.BR "argv[argc-1]" ).
.TP
.B PATH
Three consecutive NUL-terminated strings representing:
.IP 1. 3
Host path to executable binary.
.IP 2. 3
Process root directory (
.I chroot
path, or an empty string).
.IP 3. 3
Current working directory at crash time.
.TP
.B BT
ASCII text containing human-readable backtrace information recorded by the
emulator runtime at the time of termination.
.SH CHECKSUM
The container integrity is validated using standard CRC32. The checksum is
computed across the entire file contents with the 4-byte
.I crc
header field at offset 20 set to zero.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR "v4-dumpinfo" (1),
.BR "v4-disasm" (1)
